From 2bd1383046b9463e4a02a597ae5dce30ce759e42 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Prabath Kumar <prabath.karlapati@neuronsolutions.my>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 02:19:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] A tool that exits 0 is saying the thing worked

A sixth pilot, a developer told to decide whether their team could adopt
this, said no. Their words: the compiler underneath is better than the
tooling around it, and the blocker is strata repair.

They were right about all of it.

strata repair rewrote their source in place with nothing shown and nothing
kept. Their table held cost_net and cost_vat; they typed cost_xat and wanted
the net total. Repair picked cost_vat, printed "clean after 1 repair" and
exited 0, and the program then printed the VAT total. That is the silent
wrong answer this project exists to prevent, produced by its own flagship
feature.

Three changes. A near-miss is now a decision rather than a repair: the old
rule refused only an EXACT tie, which almost never happens -- cost_xat is one
character from one column and two from the other, and that is still a coin
flip on which total a program prints. Anything within a margin is refused by
name. What a repair changes is printed as a diff. And the file as it was
found is kept beside it as <file>.before-repair, named at the end of the run.

strata test exited 0 on a repository that does not compile. It only ever
built the files that HOLD verify blocks, so deleting or renaming the test
file made a broken tree report "0 passed, 0 failed" and pass. It compiles
the project now and fails with the compiler's own words if it does not
build. A project with no verify blocks at all fails too: an empty run is not
a passing run. And run outside a project it no longer falls back to a
directory called test_suite -- this repository's own -- printing find's
complaint and exiting 0; it refuses there, the way build and run always have.

The README said "Runs on physical iPhone and Android handsets" and, fifteen
lines later, "has never run on a physical handset". Both had been true; the
second was left behind on 1 October when the first stopped being false. The
pilot read both and said it cost them trust in the whole comparison table,
which is the right reaction. The stale lines are gone, and the claims gate
now fails on a list of retired claims so a sentence that was true once cannot
quietly come back.

E004 told a newcomer with a missing import to declare the table a second
time, which would give them two conflicting schemas. It was the first thing
the pilot hit. The hint now offers the import as well.

strata new ships no .gitignore and the compiler writes main.c beside
main.sta, so the first `git add .` commits 92 KB of generated C. It ships one
now, covering the generated C, the build directory and the repair backups.

Every build printed a clang warning from the compiler's own generated C --
`if ((first == 0))`, six lines of carets, on every build of any program with
an `if`. Binary expressions are generated parenthesised and `if` added a pair
of its own. One layer is dropped where it wraps the whole condition, in both
generators.

strata repair --help answered "usage: ai_self_repair.py", leaking the Python
implementation in the one tool whose README section is about not being
Python.

TUTORIAL.md section 5 told the reader to run strata test at a point where it
fails: adding a column means the scaffolded test file needs it too, and the
tutorial never said so.

New test: Exit 0 Means It Worked. Twenty-three checks over the four tools,
each one a thing that reported success when it had not succeeded.

