From 4ca46c2ccc69489a09538d96067af7ebb702a97c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Prabath Kumar <prabath.karlapati@neuronsolutions.my>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 06:15:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] One shape for a diagnostic, and a syntax error that
 points at the mistake

The sixth pilot read three different location formats in one session:
`[E004] msg (line 10, col 40)` from strata check, `E004 10:40 msg` from
strata build, and a third with the filename when the diagnostic came from an
imported module. Nothing could parse all three. The editor extension could
only ever read one of them, which is why its parser had a regex for the
check wording and nothing for the build's.

There is one shape now, everywhere: CODE file:line:col message, which is
what an editor already knows how to jump to. The record carries a file only
when the diagnostic is not in the file being compiled; each renderer fills in
the path it was given, so a record never has to hold one spelling of a path
that another part of the toolchain spells differently. The extension parses
that shape, and the documentation's examples are in it.

A syntax error pointed at the end of the file. `int broken( {` on line 13
was reported as "Expected IDENT, got '' (EOF) at line 14, col 1", because
parse_type took whatever token was there -- the brace, as a type name -- and
then ran to the end looking for the parameter. The oracle said "Expected
type, got '{'" at the brace, so this was the two parsers disagreeing as well.
A type that cannot begin a type is refused where it is.

strata_concat and str_concat both work and do the same thing, and the pilot
found both in use in our own scaffold and tutorial with nothing saying which
was meant. str_concat is the one std provides and the one to write; the
strata_* names are the runtime's own. The scaffold and the tutorial use the
std name, and FOR_DEVELOPERS says which is which.

Two findings recorded rather than fixed: an import from std is decorative --
deleting it changes nothing, because the standard library is linked into
every program, while an import from app is enforced -- and the int-to-float
widening question is still open.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01X3nmaR2hFnsQCpRArDZ78C
